# CraftLaunch — Frontend environment variables
# Copy to .env.local for local development. Never commit the real .env.local.
#
# Only NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are exposed to the browser. Never put a
# secret key, storage credential, or payment secret behind NEXT_PUBLIC_ —
# those belong on the Django backend only.

# ---- Active since Part 1 ---------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the Django API this app talks to — every request in
# src/lib/api-client.ts (including all of Part 2's auth calls) is built
# from this.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Canonical URL of this frontend deployment (used in metadata).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---- Part 6: Razorpay ------------------------------------------------------
# No NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID here, deliberately — see below.

# The public Razorpay Key ID (safe for the browser — it's not a
# secret, just an account identifier) is NOT read from a build-time
# env var. It comes back in the response body every time
# POST /api/payments/bookings/<id>/{advance,final}/order/ creates an
# order (see PaymentOrderResponseSerializer), read fresh from
# PaymentConfiguration on the backend. A NEXT_PUBLIC_ var would be
# baked in at build time — if an admin switches Sandbox/Live mode (or
# rotates keys) from Settings → Payment Settings, a statically-baked
# frontend key would silently go stale until the next redeploy, which
# is exactly what the spec's 'switching between Test and Live... 
# through the existing Admin/Deployment configuration architecture'
# is asking this NOT to require. The Razorpay Key *Secret* and the
# webhook secret never leave the backend either way — see
# backend/.env.example's Part 6 section for where those are set.

# Set only if a later part adds unsigned client-side uploads. File
# uploads are expected to go through the Django backend by default so
# storage credentials never reach the browser.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
